# BUG-124 regression fixture — BOOTSTRAP-ONLY, hence `smoke_run_bootstrap`.
#
# The bug lived in the Zig-implemented compiler's `genBoxedArgExpr`: a `^T?` constructor
# argument whose T is a VALUE type (a union or struct, not a class) was boxed with the
# NILABLE wrapper still attached -- `_allocator.create(?Val)`, giving a `*?Val` where the
# field wants `?*Val`. The fix strips the wrapper and creates the payload type.
#
# WHY THE ENTRY SAT OPEN FOR MONTHS. The selfhost has always been correct here, and
# `selfhost_smoke` runs the SELFHOST -- so no existing harness would ever have executed a
# case that could fail. The evidence was code PRESENCE (the fix is visible in the source),
# which is weaker than running the case; the 2026-08-17 triage said exactly that and kept
# the entry open rather than closing on it.
#
# THE TYPE MUST BE A VALUE TYPE. `genBoxedArgExpr` returns early for a class name -- a
# class is already a pointer -- so a class-typed field never reaches the boxing path and
# would pin nothing. `Val` is a union.
#
# WHY A CROSS-MODULE PAIR. Reading back through a SAME-MODULE `^Val?` is a bootstrap gap
# unrelated to this ticket (`var v: Val = c.opt_val!` in one file gives "expected type
# 'T', found '*T'" there, while the selfhost handles it fine). The cross-module form works
# in both, and is the shape test/val_test.zbr already uses for BUG-122. The boxing this
# ticket is about happens in the LIB's `makeContainer`, so the split costs no coverage.
#
# A STRUCT payload was in the first draft and was REMOVED, not quietly dropped: `^Pair?`
# with `PairBox(p)` fails on BOTH compilers today ("expected type '?*T', found 'T'") --
# a value-typed STRUCT is not auto-boxed into a nilable heap field the way a union is.
# That is a real gap, it is NOT BUG-124 (which is about the box's type ARGUMENT, on a
# path that does fire), and it is filed separately rather than smuggled in here.

use bug124_boxed_ctor_lib exposing Val, Container, makeContainer

def main()
    var c = makeContainer(7)
    var v: Val = c.opt_val!
    if v is Val.int_val as n
        assert n == 7

    var c2 = makeContainer(99)
    var v2: Val = c2.opt_val!
    if v2 is Val.int_val as n2
        assert n2 == 99

    # nil-ness is part of the contract too: a boxed value must read as PRESENT, not as a
    # pointer-to-optional that merely happens to be non-nil
    assert c.opt_val != nil

    print("bug124: OK")
